
## Decision24 amendment: external CPU-CI stack observation

This amendment ID is distinct from Decision23's existing numbered subclauses23-28; none is renumbered or replaced. The six actual pre-amendment files are retained byte-exact in artifacts/benchmark_recovery_20260930/decision24-planning-originals-v1. Corrected forensic reviewdb3cc89779ec00a7dd5bc31fd7e11ba8673ca776bbd6f5b176714d9a3337ca03 binds original hostedrun36687928235/job109797979763/source5f78da4c2c8dde5c9fd5c42ab8d05dcb2b589659 and its retained ZIP/log/source facts. The known watchdog/subreaper incompatibility is proven; native139 and namespace-denial causes remain unknown.

### External parent, one original full-suite child

Move periodic observation out of the full-suite interpreter. One original parent launches exactly one fresh full-suite child under the same pinned Python3.12.3 and unchanged full discovery, result logging, required native successes and original test fixture descendants. The observer is neither a child thread nor a suite-worker descendant. It must not install a native watchdog, a Python timer thread, faulthandler.register or one subprocess per test. Keep the production subreaper/WSL/native-task checks unchanged and observe actual child native tasks; imported libraries may still add native threads, which remain a real failure. No thread-count waiver or environment tweak is implied.

The parent owns the actual child PID/pidfd, start ticks, UID/GID, boot and process-group lifecycle, exact executable/argv and raw original channels. Preserve child return code/signal, original failures and incomplete-test starts; a crash or forced containment never becomes a successful suite. Preserve source-before and source-after where reachable, recording absence after interruption honestly. The same90-minute whole-job budget covers setup, observation, full suite and teardown; no fresh90-minute clock or shortened600000-ms execution/615000-ms outer broker allowance is introduced. Stop requests before original worker teardown and close/reap/drain owned resources under the existing deadline, with at most15s or remaining time for observer cleanup; lateness/unknown containment remains failed.

### Honest requests and bounded responses

After the child explicitly reports that its main-thread handler and dedicated trace FD are ready, schedule requests every60s against that original pidfd. The Python SIGUSR1 handler calls faulthandler.dump_traceback synchronously at Python dispatch with the GIL; it creates no watchdog/thread and raises no exception into the active test. Retain bounded failure facts if dumping fails. Preserve the installed signal disposition and intended syscall restart behavior, and restore/close only after requests have stopped. Reserve SIGUSR1 for this CI observer; reject a conflicting existing handler rather than silently overriding it.

Retain scheduled/actual send times, actual handler response start/end and observed arrival times on explicit clock domains. A send is not a completed dump. Record late, missing or potentially coalesced responses without inventing one-to-one delivery or timely stacks, and do not reset a test/job allowance while waiting. Long native C execution can defer Python dispatch indefinitely. The prior periodic60s-stack wording therefore means scheduled requests plus whatever original completed responses actually exist, with these limits.

Use one parent-drained pipe and one live dedicated child write FD, not a directly written regular trace file that bypasses byte limits. Check the persisted trace prefix before each write; retain at most1MiB total trace bytes. Overflow, broken capture/persistence, observer failure or unclosed/drain failure is sticky failed CI, with bounded original prefix/failure facts and only owned-child containment. Trace volume is not proved bounded by faulthandler's frame limits. Original test-event/stdout/stderr channels and immediate real failures retain their existing contracts. Host metadata stays whitelisted and <=16KiB. New observer/namespace-diagnostic metadata and trace additions use a fresh <=8MiB namespace; they do not overwrite prior outputs or grant acceptance.

Primary sources: [CPython3.12.3 watchdog creation](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/v3.12.3/Modules/faulthandler.c#L677), [Python signal dispatch/restart semantics](https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/signal.html#execution-of-python-signal-handlers), [faulthandler descriptor lifetime](https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/faulthandler.html). These explain the design limits; they do not establish the original139 cause or a Python-version fix.

### Namespace facts precede a scoped policy remedy

Before a full-suite child, at most one preselected diagnostic may exercise the original namespace setup under the same interpreter and unchanged unprivileged workload identity: <=20s execution plus <=10s or remaining cleanup, all within the same job deadline. Record actual syscall/stage/errno, original PID/executable/AppArmor label and <=64KiB timestamped relevant audit/kernel denial facts or explicit unavailable facts. Do not convert generic errno13, a sysctl value or an injected unit test into proof of an AppArmor denial or namespace success.

Only a physically joined original userns policy denial naming that exact interpreter/operation may admit a separately reviewed, ephemeral job-owned per-executable AppArmor profile. Bind observed denial, actual interpreter path/identity, minimal profile bytes and actual load/unload terminals; reject existing-profile collision and unavailable/failed enforcement, retire only the owned profile. Keep the backend unprivileged, its capability/namespace/auth/containment predicates unchanged, and verify the actual intended namespace operation afterward rather than assume policy load grants it. [Ubuntu's documented per-executable userns policy](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/release-notes/24.04/) supports this conditional route. Global sysctl changes, privilege/capability bypass, sandbox replacement, blanket skips, Python bump and automatic retry/profile search remain excluded. If no exact denial exists, retain failure/unknown facts and do not provision a profile.

### Verification and migration boundary

After exact planning review/commit/PR, implement only the CI observer and specifically demonstrated diagnostic/policy seam. Focused real-child tests must cover original one-native-task scope, normal/late/coalesced/missing responses, conflicting handlers, trace overflow/write/EOF/drain failure, original nonzero/signal/cancellation and bounded owned reap/FD cleanup. Conditional namespace tests must reject unjoined/foreign denials and profile collision/failure, preserve unavailable facts and prove no global policy/production change. Then execute the unchanged required native/full hosted checks with original outcomes/source-before/after/report, final ext4/skip and conformance gates. No known-observer repair alone proves current green CI or scientific/component acceptance, and no original failed run is restarted or reclassified. Decision23 release scope and every original unchecked campaign task remain intact.
